8. Direction semantics

null

directionalLaunch: null

means no launch direction.

A hit with a null direction must not alter the target’s launch velocity.

It can still:

* deal damage;
* increase Launch Point;
* cause whatever existing stun/hitstop behavior its hit definition specifies;
* participate in the rest of the technique.

A null direction is particularly appropriate for non-launching damage such as Charged BA2 ticks.

Even if malformed data combines a nonzero Base Launch with a null direction, null direction must result in no physical launch.

horizontal

Apply:

launch strength = Base Launch × Launch Point

horizontally, traveling in the hit’s existing facing/travel direction.

For fighter melee this uses the attacking fighter’s hit facing.

For a projectile it uses the projectile’s travel direction.

For a clone it uses the clone’s facing.

For a charged technique it uses the technique’s captured facing.

A horizontal hit should not invent vertical launch.

Conceptually:

vx = launchStrength * facing;

Do not multiply launchStrength by another horizontal tuning number.

vertical

Launch upward with exactly the computed launch strength.

The game world currently uses positive Y downward, so implementation will normally resemble:

vy = -launchStrength;
grounded = false;

Do not apply horizontal launch unless another existing non-launch movement mechanic specifically requires it.

reverseVertical

This is the exact opposite of normal vertical launch.

The target is launched downward with the computed strength.

Conceptually:

vy = launchStrength;
grounded = false;

This is a direction, not a negative Base Launch.

